 "structure": "Massachusetts History and Social Science Curriculum Framework 2018 (Pre-K-12). Front matter (cover, credits, vision, 10 Guiding Principles, scope and sequence, organization, how-to-read page) is backup; 'Standards for History and Social Science Practice, Pre-K-12' (7 practices) are standards PK-12 and are repeated as a sidebar list on each grade's introductory page. Each grade or course (Pre-K to Grade 8, then HS United States History I) has: (1) an introductory page with title, narrative, a topics list, literacy note and 'Looking Back, Looking Ahead' (title = standards, narrative/topics/looking back = examples), then (2) '<Grade> Content Standards' organised by numbered Topics with codes like [PreK.T1], [K.T2], [4.T4a], [6.T3b], [8.T5], [USI.T1] and a 'Supporting Question' (examples, unless in the same unit as a topic heading); numbered standards with a. b. c. sub-items, 'For example' text and 'Clarification Statement' (examples), 'Key/Suggested Primary Sources ... in Appendix D' (examples), and numbered footnotes (content footnotes = examples; footnotes in front matter, literacy and practice-statement notes = backup); then (3) 'History and Social Science and the Standards for Literacy' with grade-specific Reading Informational Text [RI], Writing [W], Speaking and Listening [SL] standards (Pre-K-5) or grades 6-8 [RCA-H, WCA, SLCA] literacy standards (backup). Grades 6-7 are a two-year world geography sequence labelled Grade 6 and Grade 7; Grade 8 is Civics (Topics 1-7). High school courses are titled 'United States History I' etc.; course = 'United States History I' for that course's own units, and the Practice standards sidebar is course null grades PK-12. The view is single-column text; tables are rare (a one-column 'Suggested sources' table in Grade 6). This part covers Pre-K through US History I Topic 7 up to u1192 (u1195 onward is labeled by another agent).",
